Did you know that Popeye ate spinach, because a German researcher misplaced a comma? What if AI's thirst can be explained by similar mistakes? The most widely cited paper on the thirst of AI is ("Making AI Less "Thirsty": Uncovering and Addressing the Secret Water Footprint of AI Models")[https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03271] by Pengfei Li, Jianyi Yang, Mohammad A. Islam, Shaolei Ren. It will soon be published in a special issue of (Communications of the ACM)[https://cacm.acm.org/]. It is widely cited, having 169 citations according to (Google Scholar)[https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?arxiv_id=2304.03271]. As a Dutch academic told me on LinkedIn; "It's been peer-reviewed", which apparently means it must be true. Except, I wonder if those computer scientists doing the peer-review for the  Association for Computing Machinery (ACM), have any strong background in evaluating water footprints. 

I've expressed my concerns to the corresponding author assistant professor Shaolei Ren, University of California, Riverside. Version 4 of the paper definitely addressed some of my concerns with the earlier versions. However, as I will show here, the values he uses for water withdrawal and consumption are significantly different from those published in other literature. Through the revisions between versions 1 to 4 of the paper, I do see some changes that addressed some of the concerns I expressed with earlier versions. The much higher rates for water withdrawal and consumption haven't been been fixed, because prof. Ren relies on the values published by the World Resource Institute. And that is where I think the problem starts. The data by the World Resource Institute can't be correct. They are generally much higher than academic estimates and official government statistics by as 2x, 10x, 50x and even sometimes over 100x. 

