##The different scopes of fresh water withdrawal and fresh water consumption
To understand how much water datacenters use, it is necessary to understand the difference between fresh water withdrawal and fresh water consumption and the different scopes for each of these: 
- Withdrawal: The amount of fresh water removed from the ground or diverted from a water source for use in a process. In the context of this article it could be drinking water, canal water to cool a data center, generate electricity or cool a power plant. It could also be for agriculture or drink water production. 
- Consumption: the amount of fresh water that is evaporated, transpired, incorporated into products or crops, or otherwise removed from the immediate water environment. In the context of data centers, that could be water that is evaporated when cooling the data center, or the water that evaporates when electricity is generated by a power plant that supplies the data center its water. 
The "fresh" water bit is of great importance too. It is (the lack of) fresh water that is essential to life on land and for which shortages are of great concern. Therefore in most analysis the volume of sea water isn't analysed or incorporated in analysis. A data center or power plant that withdraws 1 billion liter of fresh water for cooling is deemed a bigger issue than a data center or power plant withdrawing ten times more sea water for the same process. Even if all of the sea water withdrawn is consumend, it may not end up in the statistics, because the availability of sea water is assumed to be abundant. Unfortunately not all published data, either by firms or academics, make a good distincition between fresh water and sea water withdrawal and consumption. 
There are also two different scopes to consider when analysing the water footprint of a data center;
- Scope 1 is all the water directly consumed by the data center, which could be for cooling, but also water used for a cup of coffee or the staff bathroom. 
- Scope 2 is primarily the water consumed for generating electricity for the data center. In theory we could also include the water at the carwash to clean the company cars etc., but that is a trivial amoun and as a reuslt isn't so relevant for this analysis.  
With scope 2 we immediately get into difficult debates about how to account for it. For example imgaine a data center has purchased all its electricity from solar and wind farms with hourly matching. In sucha a case the scope 2 CO2 emissions are deemed to be 0, zero, nothing. Most analysis of data center water use however doesn't make this distinction for scope 2 water withdrawal and consumption, instead applying the average factor for the grid to a particular data center. 


